    "lawCode": "Amd §485-r, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6460\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to authorizing\na residential redevelopment inhibited property exemption\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo authorize municipalities to institute a property tax exemption on\nthe increased assessment associated with the repair and redevelopment\nof certain dilapidated residential properties to the extent that the\nassessment would appreciate as a result of the repair and\nredevelopment.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 - amends Real Property Tax Law section 485-r to delete the\nreference to certain cities and makes the section applicable to any\nmunicipality. The section further defines \"municipality\" to mean any\ncity, town or village.\n\nSection 2- provides that the act shall take effect immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn 2015, the Governor signed Chapter 406, which allowed certain\nmunicipalities to implement, at its option, a new method of\nencouraging redevelopment of dilapidated housing in the city. The city\nthat fit the specific description, the City of Batavia in Genesee\nCounty, is faced with a large number of single family homes that are\ndilapidated and in need of extensive and costly repairs.  These costs\ncan exceed the value of the home when complete, and as a result, they\nsit unused. These homes reduce the values of the houses that surround\nthem and their neighborhoods in general, and this bill will provide\nneeded incentive to make necessary redevelopment occur.\n\nThe scenario faced by the City of Batavia is far from unique. In fact,\ncities, towns and villages across the state face this same problem.\nThis bill will extend the same option to other municipalities who are\nstruggling to redevelop blighted residential areas where the cost of\npurchase and renovation will exceed the value of the homes when\ncomplete. It is in everyone's interest to improve these areas. There\nis no actual cost for these incentives, since without them the\nproperties will continue to languish with low values or off the tax\nrolls entirely, and surrounding values will continue to be depressed.\nMore importantly, these neighborhoods will provide a better place for\nits residents to live.  Inhibited Property Tax Exemptions\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015 -- S5196-A /A.7570-A- CH.406\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
